# Azure Update Manager Lab

Patch management and compliance reporting for Windows Server on Azure — Azure Update Manager, automated assessment, maintenance windows, and JSON compliance reporting. Deployed with modular Terraform, validated with Azure CLI.

---

## What You'll Learn

- Building modular Terraform infrastructure (4 independent modules)
- Using Azure Policy to auto-enroll VMs into patch assessment at scale
- Configuring a Maintenance Window — when patches run, what gets patched, reboot behavior
- Understanding the difference between assessment and patching (they are separate operations)
- Triggering on-demand patch assessments via the Azure CLI
- Writing a compliance validation script that exports machine-readable JSON

---

## What This Lab Simulates

Picture a security team that just got an alert: a critical vulnerability with a CVSS score of 9.8 was disclosed. They need to know — right now — which servers are missing the patch.

This lab builds the infrastructure and tooling to answer that question:

- A **Domain Controller (DC01)** — the identity backbone for the environment. All machines are domain-joined to `aumlab.local`.
- Two **Member Servers (WS01, WS02)** — simulate production workloads that need to be assessed and patched on a schedule.
- **Azure Policy** — automatically enrolls every VM in the resource group into periodic patch assessment. No manual enrollment per VM.
- **Maintenance Configuration** — a weekly patch window (Friday 2 AM Eastern) targeting Critical, Security, and UpdateRollup patches only.
- **Maintenance Assignments** — links the schedule to each VM. A VM that is assessed but not assigned will show compliance data but never receive automated patches.
- **validate-lab.ps1** — triggers a live assessment on each VM and exports a compliance report as JSON, ready to feed a SIEM or ticketing system.

---

## Architecture

```
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                          Azure VNet                              │
│                          10.0.0.0/16                             │
│  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐  │
│  │                    Subnet: 10.0.1.0/24                     │  │
│  │                                                            │  │
│  │   ┌──────────────┐   ┌──────────────┐   ┌─────────────┐   │  │
│  │   │     DC01     │   │     WS01     │   │    WS02     │   │  │
│  │   │   10.0.1.4   │   │  (dynamic)   │   │  (dynamic)  │   │  │
│  │   │ Domain Ctrl  │   │ Member Server│   │Member Server│   │  │
│  │   └──────────────┘   └──────────────┘   └─────────────┘   │  │
│  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘  │
│                                                                  │
│  ┌──────────────────────┐   ┌───────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │   Azure Key Vault    │   │    Azure Storage Account      │   │
│  │  (admin password)    │   │     (Terraform state)         │   │
│  └──────────────────────┘   └───────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                  │
│  ┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │              Azure Update Manager                         │   │
│  │  Azure Policy (auto-enroll) → Maintenance Config          │   │
│  │  → 3 Maintenance Assignments → JSON Compliance Report     │   │
│  └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

| VM   | Role              | OS                  | Private IP |
|------|-------------------|---------------------|------------|
| DC01 | Domain Controller | Windows Server 2022 | 10.0.1.4 (static) |
| WS01 | Member Server     | Windows Server 2022 | Dynamic |
| WS02 | Member Server     | Windows Server 2022 | Dynamic |

- **Domain:** aumlab.local
- **Region:** eastus
- **VM Size:** Standard_D2s_v3

> 💰 **Cost Estimate:** ~$0.17/hr for all three VMs. Run `terraform destroy -auto-approve` when finished to avoid unexpected charges. (~$4/day if left running)

---

## Module Structure

This lab uses four independent Terraform modules — each owns exactly one concern:

| Module | What it deploys |
|--------|----------------|
| `modules/networking` | VNet, subnet, NSG, NSG-subnet association |
| `modules/keyvault` | Key Vault (RBAC model), admin password secret |
| `modules/compute` | 3 VMs, DC promotion extension, domain join extensions |
| `modules/update-manager` | Azure Policy assignment, Maintenance Configuration, 3 Maintenance Assignments |

---

## Prerequisites

| Tool | Version | Install |
|------|---------|---------|
| Terraform | >= 1.5.0 | [Download](https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/downloads) |
| Azure CLI | Latest | `winget install Microsoft.AzureCLI` |
| PowerShell 7 | Latest | `winget install Microsoft.PowerShell` |
| Active Azure subscription | — | [portal.azure.com](https://portal.azure.com) |

**Register required Azure resource providers before deploying:**

```powershell
az provider register --namespace Microsoft.Maintenance
az provider register --namespace Microsoft.GuestConfiguration

# Wait until both show Registered
az provider show --namespace Microsoft.Maintenance --query registrationState -o tsv
az provider show --namespace Microsoft.GuestConfiguration --query registrationState -o tsv
```

---

## Pre-Setup — Remote State Backend (One Time)

Terraform keeps a record of everything it builds called a **state file**. This step stores it in Azure Blob Storage so it persists across machines and sessions.

```powershell
az group create --name RG-TerraformState --location eastus

# Name must be globally unique, 3-24 lowercase chars + numbers only
az storage account create `
  --name <YOUR_UNIQUE_NAME> `
  --resource-group RG-TerraformState `
  --sku Standard_LRS

az storage container create --name tfstate --account-name <YOUR_UNIQUE_NAME>
```

Then open `backend.tf` and replace `REPLACE_WITH_YOUR_STORAGE_ACCOUNT_NAME` with the name you chose.

> **Note:** If you already have a `RG-TerraformState` storage account from Lab 1 or Lab 2, use the same one. Just set `key = "aum-lab.tfstate"` — it will not conflict with other labs.

---

## Step 1 — Configure Variables

```powershell
Copy-Item terraform.tfvars.example terraform.tfvars
```

Open `terraform.tfvars` and set your values:

```hcl
location            = "eastus"
resource_group_name = "rg-aumlab"
admin_username      = "labadmin"
allowed_rdp_ip      = "YOUR_PUBLIC_IP/32"   # find at whatismyip.com
domain_name         = "aumlab.local"
domain_netbios      = "AUMLAB"
```

Open `modules/update-manager/main.tf` and update `start_date_time` to any future date:

```hcl
start_date_time = "2026-09-01 02:00"   # must be a future date — format: YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM
```

Set the admin password as an environment variable — it never touches disk:

```powershell
$env:TF_VAR_admin_password = "YourStrongPassword123!"

# Verify it is set — if nothing prints, set it again before running plan
echo $env:TF_VAR_admin_password
```

> **Why an environment variable?** Terraform automatically reads any `TF_VAR_*` environment variable as the matching input variable. The password exists in memory only — never written to a file, never in `terraform.tfvars`, and can never accidentally be committed to GitHub.

> **Password requirements:** Min 12 characters. Must include uppercase, lowercase, a number, and a symbol.

> ⚠️ **The password resets every time you open a new PowerShell session.** If you close and reopen the terminal, set it again before running `terraform plan` or `terraform apply`.

---

## Step 2 — Deploy Infrastructure

```powershell
az login
az account show

terraform init

# Note: flags must be quoted on Windows PowerShell
terraform plan -out="aum-lab.tfplan"

# Takes 15-20 minutes — keep this window open the entire time
terraform apply "aum-lab.tfplan"
```

After `terraform apply` completes you will see outputs like:

```
Outputs:

dc01_public_ip = "20.x.x.x"
ws01_public_ip = "20.x.x.x"
ws02_public_ip = "20.x.x.x"
key_vault_name = "kv-aum-4beef2e8"
resource_group = "rg-aumlab"
```

> **What just got deployed:** 3 VMs joined to `aumlab.local`, a VNet with RDP locked to your IP, a Key Vault holding the admin password, an Azure Policy auto-enrolling all VMs into patch assessment, a weekly Maintenance Configuration, and Maintenance Assignments linking the schedule to each VM.

> **If apply fails partway through:** Re-run `terraform apply "aum-lab.tfplan"`. Terraform skips what already succeeded and only retries what failed. Most errors are recoverable this way.

---

## Step 3 — Run Compliance Validation

The validation script triggers a live patch assessment on each VM and exports a compliance report as JSON. No pre-triggering needed — `az vm assess-patches` does assessment and result retrieval in one call.

```powershell
$subId = az account show --query id -o tsv

# Takes ~6-9 minutes (2-3 min per VM)
./scripts/validate-lab.ps1 -ResourceGroup "rg-aumlab" -SubscriptionId $subId
```

Expected output:

```
=== Azure Update Manager Compliance Validation ===
[PASS] DC01 -- Critical missing: 0 | Other: 1 | Status: Succeeded
[PASS] WS01 -- Critical missing: 0 | Other: 1 | Status: Succeeded
[PASS] WS02 -- Critical missing: 0 | Other: 1 | Status: Succeeded

Overall: ALL PASS
Report exported: aum-compliance-report.json
```

> **What the results mean:**
> - **Critical missing: 0** — no Critical or Security patches outstanding. Lab validated.
> - **Other: 1** — one non-critical patch available. Normal on fresh VMs, not required for compliance.
> - **Status: Succeeded** — assessment completed cleanly.
> - **FAIL with Critical missing > 0** — assessment is working correctly and found outstanding patches. This is expected behavior on brand-new VMs. Apply patches via the maintenance window and re-run to confirm.

The exported `aum-compliance-report.json` contains structured data per VM including assessment status, patch counts, and timestamps — ready to feed a SIEM, open tickets in ServiceNow, or archive in Blob Storage for audit history.

---

## Step 4 — Portal Verification (Optional)

Confirm everything is wired up correctly in the Azure Portal:

| Check | Where to look |
|-------|--------------|
| All 3 VMs show as Assessed | Azure Update Manager → Machines |
| aum-weekly-patches linked to all 3 VMs | Azure Update Manager → Maintenance Configurations |
| Policy applied to rg-aumlab | Policy → Assignments |

